                <pb facs="0202" n="106" sameAs="frus:frus1921v02/pg106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.01 M 31/60: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harvey</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-08-02T18:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 2,
                                1921—6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 9:31 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>634. My 570 July 9, 12 noon.<anchor n="55" xml:id="d97-f1"/> I received last
                    evening the following note from Lord Curzon dated August 1st: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>[“]My dear Ambassador: You will remember that I twice mentioned to you
                            the subject of the Asiatic mandates and have sought, to obtain from you
                            a statement of the criticisms or objections which your Government is
                            understood to desire to raise. The matter is one of some urgency since
                            the Council of the League of Nations has pressed us to give them an
                            answer before the meeting of the Assembly at the beginning of September.
                            On the other hand your Government having formally placed on record its
                            protest may be assumed to have already formulated its
                                objections.”<anchor n="56" xml:id="d97-f2"/></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harvey</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
